# ada-unlock-uefi Debian package

This package is for internal deployment. It contains locally generated NVIDIA
firmware and must not be uploaded to a public package archive.

## What apt installs

- `/usr/lib/ada-unlock/esp/` — the UEFI application and its assets. These are
  copied to the mounted EFI System Partition (usually `/boot/efi`) by
  `ada-unlockctl apply`.
- `/etc/default/ada-unlock` — the operator configuration. It is a dpkg
  conffile, so local edits survive package upgrades.
- `/usr/sbin/ada-unlockctl` — applies the configuration and removes it again.
- `/etc/default/grub.d/90-ada-unlock.cfg` — generated only when the 64 GiB BAR1
  path is selected; it appends `pci=realloc` once to the GRUB kernel command
  line.
- One UEFI boot entry, normally labelled `Ada Unlock`, and (by default) the
  first position in `BootOrder`.

## Configure

Edit `/etc/default/ada-unlock`, then run:

```sh
sudo ada-unlockctl apply
```

The main settings are:

```sh
ADA_UNLOCK_PAYLOAD="tensor"
ADA_UNLOCK_BAR1_REBAR64="false"
```

Use `tensor` for the compute unlock on both RTX 4090 and L40. For an RTX 4090
with the 64 GiB BAR1 construct, use:

```sh
ADA_UNLOCK_PAYLOAD="tensor-bar1"
ADA_UNLOCK_BAR1_REBAR64="true"
```

Then `ada-unlockctl apply` adds `pci=realloc` to GRUB and regenerates
`/boot/efi/ada-unlock.ini`. It does not run the app; the unlock happens on the
next cold boot.

`ADA_UNLOCK_SET_BOOTORDER="false"` leaves the UEFI boot order alone.
`ADA_UNLOCK_ADD_BOOT_ENTRY="false"` omits the boot entry entirely.
`ADA_UNLOCK_CHAINLOAD` is autodetected from `/EFI/debian/grubx64.efi` and the
usual fallbacks; set it explicitly if the Debian bootloader lives elsewhere.

## Remove

```sh
sudo apt purge ada-unlock-uefi
```

Before the package files are removed, `ada-unlockctl remove` deletes the UEFI
boot entry it created, restores the previous `BootOrder`, removes the GRUB
drop-in, and restores any ESP files that were overwritten during installation.
A pre-existing boot entry that the package adopted is left in place. The app
log on the ESP is intentionally left for diagnosis.

## Firmware and Secure Boot

The EFI application is unsigned. Secure Boot must be disabled. Disable the GPU
Option ROM in firmware and select onboard/BMC video as the primary display;
otherwise firmware may initialize the GPU before the app and the card is no
longer cold.
